<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elymians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Elymians were an ancient people of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> whose main towns were Segesta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a> and Entella. Greek writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> said they descended from Trojan refugees, and their language, known from short inscriptions, is still poorly understood. Often allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> against the Greek cities of Sicily, they were gradually Hellenised and absorbed into Roman Sicily.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elymians.jpg" alt="Elymians" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halibutt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sicily_prehellenic_topographic_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Elymians inhabited the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and classical antiquity. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> they were refugees from the destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. For Hellanicus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> they came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oenotrians">Oenotrians</a>. The Greek historian Philistus mentions a people of Ligurian origin without identifying it with the Elymians, and modern historians such as Heinrich Nissen and Karl Julius Beloch explored a Ligurian origin because of shared inscriptions and place names. The Elymian language is considered Indo-European, and the more generally supported opinion is that it was Italic, with similarities to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. No one has yet deciphered it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Elymians shared western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> with the Sicani, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. In the archaeological record of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> they cannot be told from their Sicani neighbours. Afterwards they adopted many aspects of the culture of the Greek colonists, raising the temple at Segesta and using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a> to write their own language.</p><p>They kept friendly relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> but often came into conflict with the Greek colonies, above all Selinus. Boundary disputes with Selinus turned to open war several times after 580 BCE. The Elymians sought an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which led to the Sicilian expedition of 415 to 413 BCE, and after its failure they encouraged the Carthaginians to attack Selinus in 409 BCE, which destroyed the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Their most important cities were Segesta, the political centre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a>, the modern Erice, a religious centre, and Entella. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> they turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. They were granted a privileged status and exempted from taxes, said to recognise their claim of Trojan ancestry, which made them kin to the Roman people, who also claimed descent from the Trojans. Under Roman rule the Elymians largely disappear from view, presumably assimilated into the general Sicilian population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elymians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As the westernmost Greek city on that coast, it faced the Phoenician towns of Panormus and Soloeis and the <b>Elymians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/himera">Himera</a></li><li><blockquote>Its necropolis shows a mixed population of Phoenicians, Greeks and <b>Elymians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/panormus">Panormus</a></li><li><blockquote>Segesta was the main town of the <b>Elymians</b>, a people of north-western Sicily who claimed Trojan descent; Thucydides says they were Trojan refugees joined by Phocians.</blockquote><a href="/state/segesta">Segesta</a></li><li><blockquote>Selinous grew rich from its grain-growing lands and trade with Carthage and the <b>Elymians</b>, and it built more large temples than almost any other Greek city: on its acropolis and on the eastern hill stand the remains of at least eight, including the colossal Temple G, and their…</blockquote><a href="/state/selinous">Selinous</a></li></ul></section>
